node_modules/
dist/
*.tsbuildinfo
packages/web/build/
packages/web/.svelte-kit/
.DS_Store
*.log
.omc/

# Git worktrees live here (see AGENTS.md — agents stay off the main tree). A worktree is a checkout of
# this repository *inside* this repository, so without this line every worktree's files show up as
# untracked changes in the main tree — and `git add -A` in a worktree would commit its siblings.
.claude/worktrees/

# The same seven names again, without the trailing slash. Not redundant: a worktree gets these as
# **symlinks** into the main tree (`.claude/worktree-bootstrap.md`), and git treats a symlink as a
# file, so `docs/` and `pki*/` below do not match one. Measured — all of them showed as untracked in
# a fresh worktree, one `git add -A` away from committing host-absolute paths into `docs`, `policy`,
# and the PKI directories. Those are exactly the site-specific material the rules further down
# exist to keep out of a published repository, so the leak has to be closed on both spellings.
#
# ⚠️ **This list and `symlinkDirectories` in `.claude/settings.json` are two halves of one thing.**
# `pki` and `pki-util` were in neither for as long as they existed: a worktree session could not read
# the fleet at all, because `pki/` is the **dev** VPC and the manager verifies client certificates
# against that CA. The symptom was not "permission denied" — it was `no client certificate`, which
# reads as a broken client. Adding a name to one list without the other reopens exactly this.
node_modules
docs
/policy
pki
pki-prod
pki-util
pki-signing

# Private keys and issued certificates. `heliopause-pki` writes these, and the natural place to run
# it is the repository root — so without these lines a CA key is one `git add -A` away from being
# published. The CA key is the trust root for the whole fleet: whoever holds it can mint an agent
# identity, and whatever an agent accepts as its relay gets to set that host's firewall.
*.key
*.pem
*.csr
*.srl
*.p12
*.pfx
*.pkcs12
*.jks
*.keystore
*.kdb
*.kdbx
*.mobileprovision
*.der
*.pk8
*.p8
pki*/
security-audits/

# Artifact directories produced by `heliopause-publish` — derived from policy/ plus a commit id, so
# committing them would create a second source of truth for what a generation contains.
artifacts/

# Soak samples from scripts/observe.sh — a local record of one run, not project state.
observe.log

# Geofeed snapshots from `heliopause-feed refresh` — third-party data, not project state.
#
# Ignored for the same reason `artifacts/` is: a snapshot is fetched, not authored, and committing it
# would create a second source of truth for what a feed said. The registration that decides *which*
# feeds exist is tracked (`src/feeds.ts`); what those URLs happened to return is not.
#
# These are not site-specific — Cloudflare's published ranges are the same for everyone — so the
# reason here is provenance, not exposure.
snapshots/

# ── Site-specific material ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Deliberately untracked, not merely tidied away. This repository is published; these files are a
# map of one specific network.
#
# `docs/` holds a deployment design for one real network: measured public addresses, per-host open
# port inventories, the operator's home egress address, and a record of which hosts were
# unprotected and when. Individually each entry is a fact worth recording. Published together they
# are a target list with addresses attached, and the most sensitive entries are the ones that make
# the record valuable — "this host serves mail publicly and had no firewall until 2026-08-01" is
# exactly what the design work needed to say and exactly what must not be public.
#
# Keeping them out of git rather than sanitising them is the choice that lasts. Sanitising means
# re-writing several hundred measurements and never being sure one was missed, then paying that cost
# again on every future measurement. Untracked, the record stays complete and honest, and there is
# no ongoing approval step where a slip publishes an address.
#
# `policy/` is the same class of thing: it names real hosts, real addresses, and the management
# source addresses that reach them.
#
# The tests belong with the policy they test. `policy/dev-runners.test.ts` imports `./dev.ts`, so
# tracking the test while ignoring its subject gives a public clone a suite that cannot run —
# verified by cloning and running it.
#
# ## Why the whole directory, not a list of files (2026-08-05)
#
# This listed the six policy files by name. That covered them exactly and was one new file away from
# failing open: `policy/staging.ts` would have been tracked by default, and nothing would have said
# so. An ignore rule for site data has to be a directory — the cost of being wrong is not symmetric.
#
# It also hides `policy/.git`. **`policy/` is now its own repository** and that is not cosmetic: the
# site module's directory is what `generationId` runs `git -C` against, so while policy sat ignored
# inside this repository every publish needed `--allow-dirty` and every generation carried a `-dirty`
# suffix — the rules running on the fleet were in no commit anywhere. Now the id is that repository's
# HEAD and names the exact policy that produced the rules. Keep the two histories apart: this one is
# published, that one is not.
#
# ## The worked example lives in `examples/`, and it cannot live here
#
# This comment used to promise `policy/example.ts` — "the tracked stand-in that shows the shape
# without describing anywhere real" — and there was no such file. The example now exists, as
# `examples/site.ts` plus `examples/site.test.ts`, and `npm test` runs it.
#
# 🔴 **It is not under `policy/`, and that is not a preference.** Operational checkouts symlink
# `policy` at the private policy repository, and git will not leave a symlink standing where it has
# to materialise a tracked path. Measured 2026-08-23: with `policy` a symlink and
# `policy/example.ts` tracked, `git checkout -- policy/example.ts` **replaces the symlink with a
# real directory and exits 0** — no warning, no error. In a live checkout that silently detaches
# 7 MB of real policy, and the first symptom is `npm test` reporting a smaller number while still
# printing green. An ordinary `git pull` would have been enough to do it.
#
# It also said to point `policyStore` in your config at a private repository. **`policyStore` has no
# consumer** — the type is declared in `src/config.ts` and read by nothing, and `heliopause-publish`
# parses no such flag. What actually works is the site module's path: publish from inside the policy
# repository and the id comes from there.
docs/
scripts/observe.targets
scripts/observe-peers.targets
observe-peers.log
# Only the checkout at the repo root. `packages/web/src/routes/policy/` is the Svelte
# console (`/app/policy/files`) and must stay tracked.
/policy/

# CPython's bytecode cache. `agent/` is standard library only and is executed rather than built, so
# these appear the moment a test **imports** one of the modules — which `agent/test_enroll.py` does.
#
# It matters more than tidiness now that `npm test` runs the Python suites too: an untracked,
# unignored `.pyc` is a binary blob, and `scan-public-history.mjs --worktree` refuses tracked or
# untracked binary blobs by policy (it cannot inspect them, so it fails closed). So a plain
# `npm test` left the leak scan red, and the message — "contains an unreviewable binary blob",
# with the path redacted by design — says nothing about which file or why.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
